ough  different stages of grief and any time  we lose some a loved one there's grief  but this is even more complicated  because you're talking about a young  person beautiful inspirational mental  health  advocate role model for the community  a person of color and this really  touches me deeply because as a  psychiatrist in the work that i do i  always ask myself what could i have done  differently right just in general and  even as a friend as a daughter i ask  myself and one thing i want to say is  don't beat yourself up you're going to  want to go back and play monday monday  morning quarterback hindsight everything  is 20 20. right oh i could have i should  have we should have done this yes all we  can do is move forward with this  information as a public health  announcement is check up on people ask  them the deep questions make time for  them distraction free eye contact even  if it's uncomfortable even if it's like  help me with this sue how does someone a  person who seem to have everything  gorgeous smart lots of friends great job  well liked how does someone like that  get to feel so alone so desperate and in  such despair you hit it on the head gail  when you said alone we are dealing with  an epidemic of loneliness and that was  before the pandemic loneliness has  nothing to do with the number of people  that you're surrounded by it has to do  with the quality of connection do you  feel understood and i think the more  famous you get it becomes harder to feel  safe it feels harder to understand  because you don't know who's got your  back and you're always competing there's  a lot of pressure there's a lot of  expectation and for women body image  issues to feel that there's a  timeline on our beauty on our youth on  our contributions and i remember reading  something about her saying a statement  that there's you know a finite limit to  this she was dealing i don't know her  and i don't know what she was dealing  with but there was a lot going on yea